{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n69 \n \n \nThis composite image shows the effects of the oxidizer flow rate on inverse flames. In these tests, we \nvaried the oxidizer flow rate from the burner while the concentrations of oxidizer and fuel in the \nchamber atmosphere were the same. These images were taken late in each test after the soot had \ndissipated. The higher flow rate resulted in a larger flame. However, a flame's radiative heat loss \nincreases with its size, so the larger flame was dimmer. This image is false colored making the flame \nappear blue and the porous spherical burner appear red. \n \nWeek 12: Dec. 31 \u2013 Jan. 6 \nOn GMT 005, Mark Vande Hei replaced the gas bottle at CIR Manifold #4 to support continuation of \nFlame Design test points. \n \n2022 \nWeek 14: Jan. 14-20 \nOn GMT 017, Raja Chari configured the ACME Chamber Insert to support the remaining ACME Flame \nDesign test points. On GMT 018, we ignited 12 inverse flames for Flame Design. We performed these \ntests with two different ethylene concentrations in a range of oxidizer concentrations and flow rates \nfrom the burner. The purpose of these tests was to use the ceramic fibers for measuring flame \ntemperature and to study flames that oscillate prior to quenching. In one test, the oscillations continued \nfor nearly 3 minutes before the flame quenched. \n \nFigure 1. Effect of Oxidizer Flow Rate on Oscillatory Quenching of Flame Design Inverse Flames \n \nFigure 1 is a preliminary graph of the oscillatory quenching of three inverse flames as a function of oxidizer \nflow rate. The measurement shown on the y-axis is of ultraviolet light from the molecule OH*, which \ntransiently occurs in combustion. This light is a good indicator of flame stability.  \n \nThe flame images show that higher oxidizer flow rates lead to larger flames, but larger flames experience \ngreater radiative heat loss. So, these larger flames quench more quickly and with fewer oscillations. The", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 69}}